        "thinking": "The user is asking whether regular plugins will work in the Cloudflare deployment. Let me think through this carefully.\n\n\"Regular plugins\" in Paperclip are plugins that:\n1. Are installed as npm packages or local paths\n2. Run as separate worker processes (using `node:child_process`)\n3. Communicate with the server via IPC/RPC mechanisms\n4. Have plugin worker managers, event buses, job schedulers, etc.\n\nWhat works in CF:\n- **Listing plugins** (`GET /api/plugins`) - ✅ reads from DB\n\n\n- **UI contributions** (`GET /api/plugins/ui-contributions`) - ✅ reads manifestJson from DB\n- **Plugin execution** - The actual plugin worker process execution uses `pluginWorkerManager` which spawns child processes. In CF Workers, this isn't directly available, but there's a sidecar Node.js server that handles this via `/sidecar/plugins/execute` and a `plugin-dispatch.ts` workflow. I'm looking at how `sidecar-client.ts` implements the executePlugin method to understand the full flow.\n\nThe execution path goes: CF Worker → Durable Object/Workflow → sidecar's `/sidecar/plugins/execute` endpoint → the sidecar actually runs the plugin worker process. So plugin execution is delegated to the sidecar, but plugin installation (which needs `node:fs` and `npm install`) and lifecycle management aren't handled there, which means the CF Worker can't fully support these operations on its own.\n\nIf a plugin was already installed on the Node.js sidecar server with its metadata in the database, I can list it and show UI contributions, but plugin management (install, uninstall, enable, disable) fails because those require filesystem access, and whether agent tools and webhooks work depends on how the sidecar endpoint is actually configured. Let me look more closely at what the sidecar client actually handles.\n\nThe real question is whether the Cloudflare deployment has a functioning sidecar running plugin workers—it's configured via `SIDECAR_URL` and `SIDECAR_API_KEY` environment variables. If those are set, plugin execution might work through the sidecar endpoint, but the sidecar still needs the plugin installed on its local filesystem. So plugins can be listed and their UI contributions displayed in Cloudflare, but actually running or managing them requires the sidecar to be running and have the plugin already installed there. Looking at the plugin dispatch workflow more carefully, it doesn't actually use the sidecar client—it pulls a bridge URL from KV storage instead.",
